# DeepSeek Harness container — optional settings.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      then edit, then `docker compose up -d`
#
# Everything here is optional: `docker compose up -d` works with no .env at
# all, and you can add your API key on the GUI's Settings page instead.
#
# This file drives LOCAL MODE (this machine only by default). For SERVER MODE
# (persistent volumes, LAN access by default), use .env.server.example /
# docker-compose.server.yml instead — or just run
#   curl -LsSf https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndreasSeidl/dsh-docker/main/scripts/install.sh | sh -s -- server

# ── Your model provider ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The key the harness talks to DeepSeek with. You can also leave this empty
# and enter the key in the web UI (it is then saved on the dsh-home volume).
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=

# Point at a compatible endpoint instead of the default api.deepseek.com.
#DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=

# ── Where the GUI is served ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The port on YOUR machine. Change it if 3080 is taken. (The GUI is served on
# 3080 inside the container — the port upstream documents — so the default maps
# it straight through. The startup banner prints this host port.)
#DSH_WEB_PORT=3080

# The address the GUI is published on — this IS the access control. The default
# 127.0.0.1 means the kernel only ever accepts connections from this machine;
# nothing on your network can reach it at all. (Server mode uses its own
# compose file, which defaults to 0.0.0.0.)
#
# FOR LAN ACCESS, set it to 0.0.0.0 (uncomment the line below). That is the
# only value that opens it up, and it is an address, not an on/off flag —
# `DSH_BIND_ADDRESS=1` fails to start with "invalid IP address: 1".
#
# There is no login screen yet, so only do this on a network you trust.
#DSH_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0

# ── What the agent works on ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# By default the agent works in a private Docker volume. Point this at a
# folder on your machine to let it read and edit your real code instead.
# The folder must be writable by the container user — see the README's
# "Work on your own code" section if edits fail with a permission error.
#DSH_WORKSPACE_DIR=./my-project

# ── Where the harness data lives ──────────────────────────────────────────
# By default the harness data (settings, credentials, chat history, plugins)
# lives on a private Docker volume. Point this at a host folder to keep it on
# disk instead — e.g. ~/.dsh, to share settings/history with a native dsh
# install you also run on this machine (note: the container user (uid 999)
# will own whatever it writes there; the startup banner tells you the exact
# chown command if it can't write).
#DSH_HOME_DIR=~/.dsh

# ── Image version ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# latest (default), a pinned release like 0.1.1-rc.2, or nightly.
#DSH_TAG=latest

# Run a locally built image instead of the published one (overrides DSH_TAG).
#DSH_IMAGE=dsh:dev

# ── Misc ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any non-empty value opts out of harness telemetry.
#DSH_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1

# Skip the app's per-run session token: DSH_WEB_AUTH_MODE=trust-proxy makes the
# bundled proxy exchange the token itself, so no 401/token URL dance for clients.
# Set it ONLY when a real access layer stands in front (a TLS + auth edge, a
# VPN, tsdproxy/Tailscale). On a plain 0.0.0.0 publish (see DSH_BIND_ADDRESS
# above) it opens the GUI to anyone who can reach the port — don't.
#DSH_WEB_AUTH_MODE=token

# ── Docker under the workspace-write permission preset (optional) ──────────
# BuildKit `docker build` and `docker login` write CLI state to ~/.docker,
# which is outside the agent's sandbox-writable roots, so they fail and the
# agent escalates (prompting you). Redirect the docker config into the
# workspace instead. The harness loads this file at launch into every bash
# call's environment; use an ABSOLUTE path (no ${VAR} expansion in .env).
# This is the invoking-directory layer, so it stays fixed until you restart
# the harness from this directory.
#DOCKER_CONFIG=/home/you/projects/dsh-container/.docker-cli
